{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And you shall come, etc.</b> It is difficult why He interrupted the promises to Abraham in the midst of the promises to the children. For even though He promises him that he will not see in his lifetime the subjugation of his seed, with all this the intention would not have been deficient had He delayed it, since even now the statement is not adjacent to the subjugation but to the redemption. Further, one needs to know why He repeated, saying ״בְּשָׁלוֹם״ ״בְּשֵׂיבָה טוֹבָה״ — and did not suffice with saying ״בְּשֵׂיבָה טוֹבָה״, in which is included as well the good tidings of Ishmael's repentance, as they said, of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 38:12), and that he would not see the subjugation of the children?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intention of the verse according to what has preceded us is that the righteous, and especially the fathers, are particular about the diminution of reward in the eternal world. Go out and learn (Genesis Rabbah 44:4) that we find he was distressed over the slaying of the kings — lest his reward from the Most High be diminished — until HaShem said to him (Genesis 15:1): ״אַל תִּירָא אַבְרָם, etc., שְׂכָרְךָ הַרְבֵּה״. Therefore, when He promised him that they would go out from the subjugation with great substance, there is room for Abraham to be concerned and to say that it would be necessary to deduct from his reward in order to perform miracles and wonders for his children and bring them out with great substance. For this reason He promised him adjacent to the saying of ״יֵצְאוּ״, etc., <b>And you shall come to your fathers in peace</b> — that nothing will be deducted from the peace which you have held fast to as a stronghold, and the kindness of trust is a gift of Elohim. And this corresponds to the promises of the soul's survival, and corresponding to the particulars of this world He said <b>you shall be buried in a good old age.</b> Here He included that he would not look upon the subjugation of his children, and also that Ishmael would do repentance, so that they would bury him in a good old age with righteous sons — for this is what the righteous hope for, and the rest of the details of good and tranquility. And herewith the difficulty of Ramban — see his words there — is resolved, for the promise did not come concerning the sojourning, etc., and the words of Ramban are forced."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:30.416821987Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}